=> Basic syntax
curl URL

Example:

curl https://example.com

This sends a request to example.com.

The server sends a response, and curl displays that response in your terminal.

=> Lab 1 — Test Internet connectivity

Run:

curl https://example.com

You may see HTML like:

<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Example Domain</title>
</head>
...
What happened?

Your Linux machine:

Linux machine
     |
     | curl request
     ↓
example.com
     |
     | response
     ↓
Linux terminal

So curl can be used to check whether your machine can communicate with a web server.

=> Lab 2 — See only HTTP headers

Use:

curl -I https://example.com

-I means show only the HTTP headers.

You may get:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html
Content-Length: 1256
Server: ...

The most important part is:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Common HTTP status codes

| Code  | Meaning                 |
| ----- | ----------------------- |
| `200` | Success                 |
| `301` | Redirect                |
| `302` | Temporary redirect      |
| `400` | Bad request             |
| `401` | Authentication required |
| `403` | Forbidden               |
| `404` | Page/resource not found |
| `500` | Server error            |
| `503` | Service unavailable     |


=> Lab 3 — Download a file

Suppose you want to download a file.

curl -O https://example.com/file.zip

-O means:

Save the file using the filename provided by the server.

For example:

file.zip

will be downloaded to your current directory.

Check:

ls -l

=> Lab 5 — Save webpage output into a file

You can use -o:

curl -o example.html https://example.com

Now check:

ls -l

You should see:

example.html

You can view it with:

cat example.html

=> Difference between -O and -o

a) curl -O URL

→ Use the original filename.

b) curl -o myfile.html URL

→ Give the downloaded file your own filename.

=> Lab 6 — Test an API

This is one of the most important uses of curl.

Try:

curl https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

You may get:

{
  "userId": 1,
  "id": 1,
  "title": "...",
  "body": "..."
}

The flow is:

Linux
  |
  | curl request
  ↓
API server
  |
  | JSON response
  ↓
Linux terminal

This is very common in DevOps and automation.

=> Lab 7 — Send data using POST

For example:

curl -X POST https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts \
-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
-d '{"title":"Linux","body":"Learning curl","userId":1}'

Here:

-X POST → use the POST HTTP method
-H → send an HTTP header
-d → send data

You don't need to memorize this now. Just understand the concept:

curl can not only receive data from a server, but can also send data to a server.

=> Very useful command for Linux admins

Suppose your company has a web server:

http://192.168.1.100

You can test it:

curl http://192.168.1.100

If it is working, you should receive a response.

You can also check headers:

curl -I http://192.168.1.100

This can help answer:

"Is the web service responding?"
